// pgbase example schema — deliberately HOSTILE, and deliberately MULTI-FILE.
//
// This is not a realistic app schema. Every model exists to exercise a metadata or query feature
// that pgbase must handle, and several exist specifically because they are known to be awkward.
// It doubles as the fixture for the differential property suite (docs/DESIGN.md §4.1), so
// anything the suite must prove correct needs a column here to prove it against.
//
// It is split across `models/` on purpose. Folder schemas are GA in Prisma 7 and are what Prisma
// itself recommends, so consumers will use them — which means pgbase's generator plugin and
// SchemaProvider must be exercised against one. A single-file fixture would ship that path
// untested. Generator and datasource blocks must stay in THIS file: Prisma requires them at the
// root of the configured schema directory, alongside `migrations/`.
//
// Coverage checklist — keep this honest when editing:
//   @@map / @map ................. models/tenancy.prisma, models/job.prisma, …
//   composite @@id ............... Membership, JobWatcher
//   PK that is also an FK ........ JobSettings (1:1 companion table)
//   self-relation ................ Thread.parent
//   implicit many-to-many ........ Job <-> Tag  (join table is invisible to the DMMF — §14.8)
//   explicit m2m via join model .. Job <-> User via JobWatcher
//   native enum .................. JobStatus, MemberRole
//   jsonb + literal default ...... Job.metadata, Task.blockedBy
//   scalar list .................. Job.labels
//   Decimal / BigInt ............. Invoice.amount / Invoice.externalRef
//   @db.Timestamptz(6) ........... every timestamp, because µs-vs-ms is divergence hazard #1
//   case-sensitivity trap ........ User.email (NOT citext — see the note there)
//   nullable everything .......... Job.closedAt, Thread.parentId — three-valued-logic hazard #3
//   tenant column ................ orgId on every client-readable model, so RLS is exercisable
//   NON-tenant model ............. AuditLog has no orgId — proves the router's wildcard bucket
//
// Partial unique indexes (Prisma cannot express these in-schema) are appended as raw SQL to the
// initial migration.

// The modern TS generator, not the legacy `prisma-client-js`. We never read `Prisma.dmmf` (it is
// absent here and gutted there — see docs/DESIGN.md §14.8), so the client generator is free to be
// the current default.
//
// NOTE: this generator picks ESM vs CJS from the nearest tsconfig's `compilerOptions.module` when
// `moduleFormat` is unset. examples/api pins `"module": "commonjs"` so `nest build` consumes the
// output without a bundler. Consumers on ESM get ESM. pgbase must work under both.
generator client {
  provider     = "prisma-client"
  output       = "../src/generated/prisma"
  moduleFormat = "cjs"
}

// Prisma 7 rejects an inline `url = env(...)` here (P1012); the connection URL lives in
// prisma.config.ts via defineConfig({ datasource: { url } }).
//
// Also note: the `prisma-client` generator is DRIVER-ADAPTER-ONLY for Postgres in Prisma 7 —
// there is no bundled query engine, and `new PrismaClient({ adapter })` is required, not
// optional. pgbase can therefore rely on the consumer already owning a `pg` Pool (§14.2).
datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}
